1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,146
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,146 --> 00:00:06,787
This is chapter ten: java dot time.

3
00:00:07,237 --> 00:00:14,426
java dot time replaced an API so badly designed that its flaws are a standard interview question.

4
00:00:14,426 --> 00:00:18,948
The replacement is good, but only if you pick the right type.

5
00:00:18,948 --> 00:00:24,581
Most date bugs in modern Java are a LocalDateTime where an Instant belonged.

6
00:00:25,481 --> 00:00:27,712
Here's a delivery slot in Java 7.

7
00:00:27,712 --> 00:00:29,606
Four defects in a few lines.

8
00:00:29,606 --> 00:00:32,852
Months are zero-based, so eight means September.

9
00:00:32,852 --> 00:00:36,368
Date is mutable, so callers can change it under you.

10
00:00:36,368 --> 00:00:37,923
Date isn't even a date.

11
00:00:37,923 --> 00:00:41,304
It's a millisecond instant with a misleading name.

12
00:00:41,304 --> 00:00:43,942
And SimpleDateFormat isn't thread-safe.

13
00:00:43,942 --> 00:00:50,569
A shared static formatter silently produces wrong output, one of the classic Java production bugs.

14
00:00:51,019 --> 00:00:56,794
In Java 8, it's a ZonedDateTime built from a LocalDate, a LocalTime and a ZoneId.

15
00:00:56,794 --> 00:01:00,573
Months are one-based, and you can use the Month enum.

16
00:01:00,573 --> 00:01:06,848
Everything is immutable, the types say what they mean, and the formatter is thread-safe.

17
00:01:07,748 --> 00:01:13,473
The most useful distinction in this API is machine time versus human time.

18
00:01:13,473 --> 00:01:16,567
Machine time is a point on the timeline.

19
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:24,303
An Instant is nanoseconds since the epoch in UTC, and a Duration is an exact amount of elapsed time.

20
00:01:24,753 --> 00:01:28,189
Human time is what a calendar and a clock show.

21
00:01:28,189 --> 00:01:35,355
LocalDate, LocalTime, LocalDateTime, and Period, a calendar amount like two months and three days.

22
00:01:35,805 --> 00:01:38,648
And the bridge between them is a zone.

23
00:01:38,648 --> 00:01:43,811
ZonedDateTime is human time plus a region with daylight-saving rules.

24
00:01:43,811 --> 00:01:46,429
OffsetDateTime uses a fixed offset.

25
00:01:46,879 --> 00:01:48,486
Here's the key warning.

26
00:01:48,486 --> 00:01:52,469
LocalDateTime has no time zone, so it is not a timestamp.

27
00:01:52,469 --> 00:01:54,914
You can't compare two across zones.

28
00:01:54,914 --> 00:02:00,852
Storing one for an event that happened is the most common mistake in this whole area.

29
00:02:00,852 --> 00:02:07,210
It's only right for recurring wall-clock ideas, like the warehouse opens at nine every day.

30
00:02:08,110 --> 00:02:10,266
Pick each type by what it means.

31
00:02:10,266 --> 00:02:14,511
placedAt is an Instant, because it's a fact about the timeline.

32
00:02:14,511 --> 00:02:19,902
deliveryDate is a LocalDate, because the customer picked a date, not an instant.

33
00:02:19,902 --> 00:02:27,246
And the slot's start is a LocalTime with a zone, because it means two thirty every day, in a particular city.

34
00:02:27,696 --> 00:02:32,031
The rule for the whole system: store and transmit Instants.

35
00:02:32,031 --> 00:02:36,733
Convert to a ZonedDateTime only at the last moment, for display.

36
00:02:36,733 --> 00:02:42,096
Convert early, and every comparison downstream has to reason about zones.

37
00:02:42,996 --> 00:02:45,723
Daylight saving is where the bugs live.

38
00:02:45,723 --> 00:02:49,219
India has no daylight saving, so let's use London.

39
00:02:49,219 --> 00:02:55,092
Start at two pm on the twenty-eighth of March, the day before the clocks go forward.

40
00:02:55,542 --> 00:02:59,510
Add a Period of one day, and you get two pm the next day.

41
00:02:59,510 --> 00:03:00,971
Same wall-clock time.

42
00:03:00,971 --> 00:03:05,078
That's what a delivery scheduled for tomorrow at two needs.

43
00:03:05,528 --> 00:03:12,737
Add a Duration of twenty-four hours, and you get three pm, because an hour vanished when the clocks changed.

44
00:03:12,737 --> 00:03:16,676
That's right for a token that expires in twenty-four hours.

45
00:03:16,676 --> 00:03:17,810
Both are correct.

46
00:03:17,810 --> 00:03:19,947
They answer different questions.

47
00:03:19,947 --> 00:03:24,152
Use the wrong one and you get a bug that shows up twice a year.

48
00:03:25,052 --> 00:03:29,518
When the clocks spring forward, some wall-clock times don't exist.

49
00:03:29,518 --> 00:03:37,164
One thirty on the twenty-ninth of March never happens in London, so ZonedDateTime moves it forward to two thirty.

50
00:03:37,164 --> 00:03:42,983
When the clocks fall back, one thirty happens twice, and the earlier offset is chosen.

51
00:03:42,983 --> 00:03:47,111
withLaterOffsetAtOverlap picks the second occurrence instead.

52
00:03:47,561 --> 00:03:48,899
Neither case throws.

53
00:03:48,899 --> 00:03:56,062
That's usually what you want, but it means a scheduling bug produces a wrong time rather than an exception.

54
00:03:56,062 --> 00:04:02,153
If a gap should be an error in your domain, check the zone rules' valid offsets explicitly.

55
00:04:03,053 --> 00:04:07,878
Calendar questions that people used to write loops for are one-liners.

56
00:04:07,878 --> 00:04:13,185
TemporalAdjusters give you next Monday, or the last day of the billing month.

57
00:04:13,185 --> 00:04:19,320
Java 9's datesUntil streams every date in a range, so excluding Sundays is just a filter.

58
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:23,042
And ChronoUnit dot DAYS dot between counts whole days.

59
00:04:23,492 --> 00:04:25,718
Careful with Period dot between.

60
00:04:25,718 --> 00:04:32,813
From the thirty-first of January to the first of March is one month and one day, not twenty-nine days.

61
00:04:32,813 --> 00:04:35,526
It describes the gap in calendar terms.

62
00:04:35,526 --> 00:04:38,169
When you want a count, use ChronoUnit.

63
00:04:39,069 --> 00:04:44,124
To make time testable, don't call Instant dot now with no arguments.

64
00:04:44,124 --> 00:04:47,840
Every now method in java dot time accepts a Clock.

65
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:53,043
Inject one through the constructor, and call Instant dot now of clock.

66
00:04:53,493 --> 00:05:03,585
In a test, pass Clock dot fixed at nine am on the twentieth of September, and you can assert the delivery date is exactly the twenty-second.

67
00:05:03,585 --> 00:05:08,342
No static mocking needed, and time becomes an explicit dependency.

68
00:05:08,342 --> 00:05:11,370
In production, inject Clock dot systemUTC.

69
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:14,068
Three internals that bite.

70
00:05:14,068 --> 00:05:15,727
Everything is immutable.

71
00:05:15,727 --> 00:05:19,945
So calling date dot plusDays one as a statement does nothing.

72
00:05:19,945 --> 00:05:24,025
The result is thrown away, and the compiler won't warn you.

73
00:05:24,475 --> 00:05:25,191
Precision.

74
00:05:25,191 --> 00:05:30,488
An Instant holds nanoseconds, but Postgres timestamptz keeps microseconds.

75
00:05:30,488 --> 00:05:34,927
Round-trip through the database and equality assertions break.

76
00:05:34,927 --> 00:05:39,365
Truncate to micros before persisting, so the loss is explicit.

77
00:05:39,815 --> 00:05:40,515
And zones.

78
00:05:40,515 --> 00:05:46,458
For a future event, store the zone ID, like Europe slash London, plus the local time.

79
00:05:46,458 --> 00:05:54,919
Store a fixed offset instead, and when a government changes its daylight-saving rules, which happens, your meeting moves.

80
00:05:55,369 --> 00:06:05,844
For legacy code, JDBC 4.2 reads and writes java dot time types directly, so convert Date and Timestamp only at the boundary.

81
00:06:06,744 --> 00:06:08,010
Production rules.

82
00:06:08,010 --> 00:06:10,094
Store and transmit Instants.

83
00:06:10,094 --> 00:06:12,476
Use LocalDate for genuine dates.

84
00:06:12,476 --> 00:06:13,593
Inject a Clock.

85
00:06:13,593 --> 00:06:17,911
Period for calendar arithmetic, Duration for elapsed time.

86
00:06:17,911 --> 00:06:19,772
ZoneId for future events.

87
00:06:19,772 --> 00:06:24,165
Declare formatters static final, since they're thread-safe.

88
00:06:24,165 --> 00:06:28,483
And use the Month and DayOfWeek enums instead of integers.

89
00:06:28,933 --> 00:06:31,842
Never use LocalDateTime as a timestamp.

90
00:06:31,842 --> 00:06:36,095
Never use Date, Calendar or SimpleDateFormat in new code.

91
00:06:36,095 --> 00:06:38,855
Never discard the result of plusDays.

92
00:06:38,855 --> 00:06:43,108
Never rely on the JVM's default time zone in server code.

93
00:06:43,108 --> 00:06:46,539
Never store a fixed offset for a future event.

94
00:06:46,539 --> 00:06:49,897
Never use Duration of days for calendar days.

95
00:06:49,897 --> 00:06:54,746
And never assume nanoseconds survive a trip through the database.

96
00:06:55,646 --> 00:07:02,046
Java 8 delivered java dot time, plus methods to convert to and from the legacy types.

97
00:07:02,496 --> 00:07:06,784
Java 9 added datesUntil and better Duration arithmetic.

98
00:07:07,234 --> 00:07:14,466
Java 12 added toHoursPart and friends, so you can format a duration without manual modulo maths.

99
00:07:14,916 --> 00:07:19,567
Java 16 added locale-correct day periods, like in the morning.

100
00:07:20,017 --> 00:07:27,902
And every release ships time zone database updates, because zone rules change by law several times a year.

101
00:07:27,902 --> 00:07:31,323
That's a real reason to keep your JDK current.

102
00:07:32,223 --> 00:07:32,892
To recap.

103
00:07:32,892 --> 00:07:39,503
Separate machine time, Instant and Duration, from human time, the Local types and Period.

104
00:07:39,503 --> 00:07:41,955
LocalDateTime is not a timestamp.

105
00:07:41,955 --> 00:07:46,264
Keep Instants in the core and add a zone only for display.

106
00:07:46,264 --> 00:07:51,167
Period means tomorrow at two, Duration means in twenty-four hours.

107
00:07:51,167 --> 00:07:55,178
Inject a Clock, and store a zone ID for future events.

108
00:07:55,628 --> 00:07:58,867
That completes the Java 8 chapters of the handbook.

109
00:07:58,867 --> 00:08:02,614
The full chapter, with interview questions, is on the site.

110
00:08:02,614 --> 00:08:03,884
Thanks for watching.
